  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "t41",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "excerpt": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "text": "clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-07T22:12:14.764Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T22:13:39.767Z",
          "html": "<p>What T41 means, in plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    A library (say, a cards library) can make things of its own kinds: its deck makes cards with <code>a new card</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    When Solitaire uses that library, you'd want the deck to make <em>Solitaire's</em> card (with Solitaire's own looks and\n    rules), not the library's plain one.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Since P26 that happens, but only when the library's project file lists the type in its <code>exports</code>:\n    <code>\"exports\": [\"Card\", \"Deck\"]</code>. Without that line, the deck keeps making the library's own plain cards.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    I chose that so no existing project's compiled code changed. Making it the rule for every type would have changed 5\n    files in recipe-box, recetario, live-room and sala-en-vivo, for no gain.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The follow-up question T41 holds: is \"only what <code>exports</code> lists\" the rule you want, or should a library always let the project using it supply its own version of each type?</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes unless you pick the other rule;  then every library type is made by name, and those 5 files' compiled code changes.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "p-upgrading-old-spell#field-3",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "Upgrading Old Spell",
      "excerpt": "Your words (2026-11-18, on data's reference pages page, sent as r31 from data's",
      "text": "we should support \"deprecations\" which have a semantic version number when they will be dropped\n\nOK to just rewrite for now",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T13:07:34.597Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "with a quick fix",
      "offset": 1175,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T13:08:09.715Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T13:11:15.604Z",
          "html": "<p>Folded into P68's plan, marked in orange:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Each old form is now a deprecation, with the Spell version it was deprecated in and the version that drops it (next major by default, e.g. deprecated in 0.9, dropped in 1.0).</li>\n  <li>The warning names both versions; from the drop version on, the same form is an error, its fix still offered.</li>\n  <li>For now the fix simply rewrites the old form to the new one, nothing fancier.</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T13:09:26.941Z"
      }
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "p-upgrading-old-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "Upgrading Old Spell",
      "excerpt": "First, your answers: Q79, Q80, Q81 and Q82 are answered (2026-11-19); only Q83 (",
      "text": "Would be great if the deprecation warning provided a \"fix it\" button",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T13:09:55.870Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Verify; speed test",
      "offset": 3380,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T13:10:11.445Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T13:11:16.364Z",
          "html": "<p>Folded into P68's plan, marked in orange:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The deprecation warning carries its fix as a \"Fix it\" button, where the warning shows: the editor's problem popup and VS Code's hover (the warning's preferred quick fix, one click).</li>\n  <li>Checks and Verify now press it: the line is rewritten and the warning goes.</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
